adaptedMonomialOrder -- constructs a ring with monomial order adapted to an integer program
*******************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        adaptedMonomialOrder(A, c)
        adaptedMonomialOrder(A, c, Variables=>L)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, with nonnegative entries in
        *note ZZ: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
      * c, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix, or a *note list:
        (Macaulay2Doc)List,, with entries in *note ZZ: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, optionally, a list of length two
        containing strings that we use as variable names in the ring
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Field: Field, => ..., default value QQ, field over which to
        define polynomial rings
      * Variables => ..., default value {w, z}
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, equipped with a term order adapted
        to the given integer program

Description
===========

Solving integer linear programs using Gröbner bases relies on constructing a
monomial order that is adapted to the program at hand. This method returns a
ring equipped with such an order.



Definition. Fix a matrix $A \in \mathbb Z^{m \times n}$ and vectors $b \in
\mathbb Z^m$, $c \in \mathbb Z^n$. Consider the integer linear program in *note
standard form: toStandardForm,: $$\text{minimize } c^Tx \quad \text{subject to
} Ax = b \text{ and } x \in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}^n.$$ Let $\varphi$ be as in *note
isFeasiblePoint: isFeasiblePoint,. A monomial order $<$ on $k[z_1, \ldots, z_m,
w_1, \ldots, w_n]$ is adapted to the program if both of the following hold:

1. (Elimination) $z_i > w^\alpha$ for all $\alpha \in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}^n$, for
all $i = 1, \ldots, m$;

2. (Compatibility with the program) if $c^T \alpha > c^T \beta$ and
$\varphi(w^\alpha) = \varphi(w^\beta)$, then $w^\alpha > w^\beta$.



Construction. We outline here the construction of such an order, following
Exercise 7 in [CLO, Chapter 8, Section 1].

First, suppose that the vector $c$ defining the objective function contains
only nonnegative entries. Define a weight order on the variables $w_1, \ldots,
w_m$ by giving weight $c_j$ to $w_j$. We extend this to an adapted monomial
order on $k[z_1, \ldots, z_n, w_1, \ldots, w_m]$ by treating each $z_i$ as
lexicographically larger than every $w_j$.

Assume now that $c$ is not nonnegative. For each $j = 1, \ldots, n$, define
$d_j = \sum_{i=1}^m A_{i,j}$. (Note that $d_j > 0$ for otherwise the
constraints in the linear program do not depend on $x_j$.) Equip $k[z_1,
\ldots, z_m, w_1, \ldots, w_m]$ with nonstandard grading: $\deg(z_i) = 1$ for
all $i$ and $\deg(w_j) = d_j$ for all $j$. Now for the term order, pick $\mu$
such that the vector $(c_1, \ldots, c_n) + \mu(d_1, \ldots, d_n)$ contains only
nonnnegative entries. This gives rise to a weight order on $k[w_1, \ldots,
w_n]$ which satisfies condition (2) above. We extend this to an adapted
monomial order on $k[z_1, \ldots, z_n, w_1, \ldots, w_m]$ by again treating
each $z_i$ as lexicographically larger than every $w_j$.



References
==========

[CLO] David A. Cox, John Little, and Donal O'Shea. Using Algebraic Geometry.
Second edition. Graduate Texts in Mathematics, 185. Springer, New York, 2005.



See also
========

  * *note solveILP: solveILP, -- solve an integer linear program in standard
    form
  * *note toStandardForm: toStandardForm, -- converts an LP to standard form
  * *note Field: Field, -- field over which to define polynomial rings

