isFeasiblePoint -- whether a point is feasible to an integer linear program
***************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        isFeasiblePoint(A, b, X)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, with nonnegative entries in
        *note ZZ: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
      * b, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix, or a *note list:
        (Macaulay2Doc)List,, with nonnegative entries in *note ZZ:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
      * X, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix, or a *note list:
        (Macaulay2Doc)List,, with nonnegative entries in *note ZZ:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Field: Field, => ..., default value QQ, field over which to
        define polynomial rings
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, 

Description
===========

Fix a matrix $A \in \mathbb Z^{m \times n}$ and integer linear program in *note
standard form: toStandardForm, with constraints $Ax = b$. The following is a
characterization of when the vector $x \in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}^n$ satisfies $Ax =
b$, in which case we say that it is feasible. This condition is exactly [CLO,
Chapter 8, Proposition 1.6].



Proposition. Let $A \in \mathbb Z^{m \times n}$ and consider the feasible
region $Ax = b$. Define a homomorphism of rings $\varphi:k[w_1, \ldots, w_n]
\to k[z_1, \ldots, z_m]$, where $k$ is any *note field: Field,, by $$\varphi:
w_j \mapsto \prod_{i=1}^m z_i^{A_{i, j}} \quad \text{for all } j = 1, \ldots,
n.$$ Then, a vector $x \in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}^n$ satisfies $Ax = b$ if and only
if $\varphi(w^x) = z^b$.



+--------------------------------------------+
|i1 : A = matrix{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}             |
|                                            |
|o1 = | 1 2 |                                |
|     | 3 4 |                                |
|                                            |
|              2       2                     |
|o1 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                      |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i2 : b = matrix{{4}, {10}}                  |
|                                            |
|o2 = | 4  |                                 |
|     | 10 |                                 |
|                                            |
|              2       1                     |
|o2 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                      |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i3 : isFeasiblePoint(A, b, {1, 1})          |
|                                            |
|o3 = false                                  |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isFeasiblePoint(A, b, matrix{{2}, {1}})|
|                                            |
|o4 = true                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+

References
==========

[CLO] David A. Cox, John Little, and Donal O'Shea. Using Algebraic Geometry.
Second edition. Graduate Texts in Mathematics, 185. Springer, New York, 2005.



Caveat
======

Assumes the program is *note in standard form: toStandardForm,.



See also
========

  * *note solveILP: solveILP, -- solve an integer linear program in standard
    form
  * *note Field: Field, -- field over which to define polynomial rings
  * *note IsInStandardForm: IsInStandardForm, -- specify whether the integer
    program is in standard form

